<!-- ===== Footer team roster: LOCKED AT 8 ACROSS ===============================
     ROOT CAUSE (found 2026-08-19): the registered script PublishPageLayoutFixes
     v1.0.0, applied to the PUBLISH PAGE ONLY, injected a <style> at runtime with
         .p-footer_team-roster{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,36px)!important}
     Because it is injected into <head> AFTER this block, an equally-important
     single-class rule here LOST to it. And auto-fill means the column count
     tracks container width - which is exactly why it looked like it "kept
     reverting" while the Designer and the published stylesheet both said 8.
     The script was replaced by PublishPageLayoutFixesV2, which keeps its aspect
     ratios and the .year shim but no longer touches grid-template-columns.

     This rule is the belt-and-braces guard. It uses a DESCENDANT selector on
     purpose: (0,2,0) beats any (0,1,0) rule regardless of source order, so a
     future injected !important one-class rule cannot win again. Do not "simplify"
     it back to a single class.

     If the roster width must ever change, change it HERE - editing only the
     Designer will look ignored, because this keeps winning. -->
<style>
  .p-footer_team-wrapper .p-footer_team-roster {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(8, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
  }
</style>

<!-- ===== Choose-path cards below desktop ======================================
     1. THE PIXEL SEAM. On desktop the band sits on the edge of the image NEAREST
     THE TEXT - left edge when the image is on the right (.is-image-right +
     overlay .is-left), right edge when the image is on the left. At 991 and below
     the card stacks text ABOVE image, so "nearest the text" becomes the image's
     TOP edge; the band stayed pinned to a side, which is why it read as being on
     the wrong side on a phone. It becomes a full-width 48px band across the top,
     matching the publish page's icon-tagline cards. Note the card starts stacking
     at 991, not 767, so this has to cover the tablet range or the bug just moves
     up a breakpoint.

     Done here rather than on the .is-horizontal combo because that combo is also
     used by cards outside this section; hence the .p-section_choose-path scope.
     Specificity is (0,4,0) so it beats .icon-tagline_card-image-overlay
     .is-horizontal.is-left at (0,3,0) without !important.

     2. THE EMPTY BLOCK OF CARD COLOUR under the Publish shot. Both coded shots
     were built from full-page captures, and each build's stylesheet still ships
         @media (max-width:767px){ .cpp, .cpp body { height:100dvh } }
     (.cpe for the Export shot). The mount div carries that namespace class, so on
     a phone it was forced to a full viewport height - 844px - while the shot's
     own stage measured 365px. The 479px difference is the empty pink you could
     see. The shot's own sizing math is fine and responsive; nothing about the
     crop needed touching. Height auto simply lets the mount collapse onto it.

     3. THE EXPORT SHOT IS BOTTOM-ALIGNED. That mount is `position: absolute;
     top: 0` set INLINE by export-asset-v7.js, so unlike the Publish shot it does
     not respect the wrapper's 48px padding-top - it sat under the pixel band,
     which clipped the floating Figma/Webflow/React/Claude tiles, and left 48px of
     empty blue at the bottom where the in-flow placeholder still held the wrapper
     open. Pinning it to bottom:0 drops it clear of the band and closes the gap.
     `!important` is required: the script writes top inline. Publish card's shot
     is in flow and needs none of this.

     Everything else about these cards - padding, stacking order, corner radii,
     the icon-left item layout - is Webflow Designer style on the choose-path_*
     classes at the `medium` and `small` breakpoints. -->
<style>
  @media screen and (max-width: 991px) {
    .p-section_choose-path .choose-path_image-wrapper .icon-tagline_card-image-overlay.is-horizontal {
      top: 0;
      right: 0;
      bottom: auto;
      left: 0;
      width: auto;
      height: 48px;
      flex-direction: column;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
    }
    .p-section_choose-path .choose-path_image-wrapper .p-pixel-row.is-stacked {
      flex-direction: row;
    }
    .p-section_choose-path .choose-path_image-wrapper .cpp,
    .p-section_choose-path .choose-path_image-wrapper .cpe {
      height: auto;
    }
    .p-section_choose-path .choose-path_image-wrapper .cpe {
      top: auto !important;
      bottom: 0 !important;
    }
  }
</style>

<!-- ===== Showcase / Demo Project cards =========================================
     SITE LEVEL ON PURPOSE. The Demo Project Card component renders on four pages
     (publish, new-homepage-publish, publish-duplicate, export). These rules and
     the counter script previously lived in the PUBLISH PAGE's own head, so the
     hover reveal and the clone counter silently did nothing on the other three -
     the component was shared, the code was not. Do not move this back to a page.
     See rules/custom-code-placement.md.

     The script is loaded with defer rather than from the footer so it still runs
     after parse; it is the registered ShowcaseCardLikes v2.2.0 build, referenced
     by URL because the site is at Webflow's hard cap of 15 applied site scripts.
     ========================================================================= -->
<style>
  /* The actions overlay ships at opacity 0 with nothing to bring it back, so
     hover reveals it; focus-within covers keyboard users. */
  .p-showcase_card-actions { transition: opacity 200ms ease-in; }
  .p-showcase_card:hover .p-showcase_card-actions,
  .p-showcase_card:focus-within .p-showcase_card-actions { opacity: 1; }

  /* Land-book's lift, on the asset only - per the design the card never casts a
     shadow. Their ramp is 70% grey, which LIGHTENS a dark page and read as a
     white glow in dark mode; these are the same layers in black at 0.263x the
     alpha, identical on the light card, still a shadow on #101010. */
  .p-showcase_card-image-wrapper { transition: box-shadow 200ms ease-in; }
  .p-showcase_card:hover .p-showcase_card-image-wrapper {
    box-shadow:
      0 .2px .2px rgba(0,0,0,.071), 0 .6px .6px -.5px rgba(0,0,0,.065),
      0 1.1px 1.1px -1px rgba(0,0,0,.06), 0 1.9px 1.8px -1.5px rgba(0,0,0,.055),
      0 3.3px 3.2px -2.1px rgba(0,0,0,.05), 0 5.6px 5.4px -2.6px rgba(0,0,0,.026),
      -.1px 8.8px 8.4px -3.1px rgba(0,0,0,.026), -.1px 13.3px 12.8px -3.6px rgba(0,0,0,.021);
  }

  /* The design puts the author name on foreground/primary; the shared link class
     resolves to secondary. Scoped to the card so no other link shifts. */
  .p-showcase_card-designer p .p-text-style-link {
    color: var(--_publish-colors---foreground--primary);
  }

  .p-showcase_card-likes { cursor: pointer; -webkit-user-select: none; user-select: none; }
  .p-showcase_card-likes .p-icon { transition: transform 150ms ease-out, color 150ms ease-out; }
  .p-showcase_card-likes[aria-pressed="true"] .p-icon { color: #ff00f4; }
  .p-showcase_card-likes:active .p-icon { transform: scale(.86); }
  .p-showcase_card-likes:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid #ff00f4; outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 6px;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .p-showcase_card:hover { transform: none; }
    .p-showcase_card-likes .p-icon { transition: none; }
  }
</style>
<script defer src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6177739448baa66404ce1d9c/6a8400505f055a6f926515f1_showcase-likes-v22.js" crossorigin="anonymous" integrity="sha384-Jf7K59K/UvRPUIFED5pGnYL7v2KjQyARZ9djnWoir1vUeSKrH9i3oDV/N4o+Rlbz"></script>
<script>
/* Preview arrow opens the live demo in a new tab.
   It cannot be set in the Designer: the href arrives via CMS field -> card's
   Site URL prop -> nested Button component's Link prop, and a CMS Link field
   carries no open-in-new-tab flag, so the anchor renders with no target. The
   Button component is shared site-wide, so setting it there would change every
   button on the site; component instances reject attributes outright. Hence JS,
   scoped to the demo arrow's own wrapper class.
   The Clone button is deliberately left alone - it stays in the same tab. */
(function () {
  function mark() {
    document.querySelectorAll('.is-demo-arrow a.p-button-link').forEach(function (a) {
      if (!a.target) { a.target = '_blank'; a.rel = 'noopener noreferrer'; }
    });
  }
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', mark);
  } else {
    mark();
  }
})();
</script>

<!-- ===== Pricing logos: invert to light in dark mode ==========================
     Dark mode is the p-theme-dark class the theme scripts put on <body>. Webflow
     classes cannot express that descendant relationship, hence CSS here.

     THE FILTER MUST SIT ON .p-logo_wrapper, NOT ON THE IMG. The wrapper carries
     filter: brightness(0) (Designer style) to normalise every logo to solid
     black, and a parent's filter composites AFTER its child's - so an invert on
     the img is flattened straight back to black and looks like it did nothing
     (verified live on staging 2026-08-23). brightness(0) invert(1) keeps the
     normalisation, then flips the black result to white.

     Scoped to .p-section_pricing-logos (the Pricing Logos component, Publish
     group) so the shared .p-logo_wrapper class is untouched elsewhere. SITE
     LEVEL per rules/custom-code-placement.md - the component is shared,
     page-scoped code would silently miss other pages that use it. -->
<style>
  body.p-theme-dark .p-logo_wrapper {
    filter: brightness(0) invert(1);
  }
</style>

<!-- ===== Footer logo: invert in dark mode =====================================
     Nothing was swapping it. .p-footer_logo is a plain <img> pointing at
     relume-logo.svg, and the compiled stylesheet carries exactly one matching
     rule - .p-footer_logo-link, for margins. So a near-black wordmark sat on a
     near-black ground on all 21 pages that render the footer.

     A filter is correct here rather than a second asset: the mark is two paths,
     both fill #101010, no gradient. Contrast the Untitled UI testimonial logo,
     which needs the .is-logo-light / .is-logo-dark swap precisely because a CSS
     filter cannot recolour part of a gradient.

     brightness(0) normalises to solid black first so the rule survives the
     source fill being changed later; invert(1) then gives pure white. Same
     formula as the pricing logos above, deliberately.

     SITE LEVEL per rules/custom-code-placement.md - the footer is shared. -->
<style>
  body.p-theme-dark .p-footer_logo {
    filter: brightness(0) invert(1);
  }
</style>
<!-- Finsweet Attributes -->
